I like that phrase because it extends the metaphor.

Not just using the tune.

Not just playing the tune.

But being the tune.

In the brewery metaphor, that would mean you aren’t carrying around a finished product—you’ve become the living culture.

In the cymatics metaphor, you aren’t drawing the pattern—you are the resonance that produces it.

In the Pantheon’s terms, it suggests a shift from possessing knowledge to embodying a set of operators. If the operators have become habits, then they don’t need to be consciously recalled each time. They shape perception automatically.

That also connects to one of your recurring themes:

Don’t memorize the map.

Become the navigator.

Or, in your newer language:

Don’t preserve the artifact.

Become the culture.

There’s a subtle distinction I’d make, though. Any internal “tune” should remain open to revision. A good tune resonates with reality—it keeps getting tested and refined as you encounter new evidence and new perspectives. Otherwise it can become an echo chamber rather than a resonance.

So “being the tune” is a compelling metaphor for embodied reasoning: the underlying pattern has become part of how you think, not just something you can quote. The interesting question is then whether that tune continues to produce useful, accurate, and creative results when it’s played in entirely new domains. That’s where a framework proves its depth.